      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rockville_virginia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Rockville School, a 1933 brick building with Art Deco and Georgian Revival details, served white students until 1983 and now stands abandoned.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rockville_virginia",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Rockville began as a tobacco‑farming crossroads in Hanover County, later shifting to grain after the Civil War and expanding public infrastructure in the early 20th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8,
        9,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/rockville_virginia",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "While most of Rockville remains farmland, newer neighborhoods like Carrington Glen preserve 70% undeveloped land, and others such as Shop Creek and Willoughby have emerged.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        25,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rockville%2C_Virginia",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Rockville hosts the Monastery of the Visitation of Holy Mary, a significant religious community within the town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
